\ prelude.fr — fr's standard library: words derivable from the kernel primitives,
\ written in fr itself. Keeping these out of fr.s keeps the assembly kernel small
\ and auditable; load the prelude for the full vocabulary:
\
\     ( cat prelude.fr yourprog.fr ) | ./fr
\
\ As fr grows, the rule is: if a word can be defined in fr, it belongs here, not
\ in fr.s. (anvil.fr depends on this prelude.)

\ --- derived stack + arithmetic words (moved out of fr.s) --------------------
: 1+    ( n -- n+1 )      1 + ;
: 1-    ( n -- n-1 )      1 - ;
: 2dup  ( a b -- a b a b ) over over ;
: 2drop ( a b -- )        drop drop ;
: nip   ( a b -- b )      swap drop ;

\ --- output ------------------------------------------------------------------
: cr    ( -- )   10 emit ;
: space ( -- )   32 emit ;

\ u. — print an unsigned number with no trailing newline, by extracting decimal
\ digits with /mod into a buffer and typing them. The kernel's `.` always adds a
\ newline; u. is the building block for inline / formatted numeric output.
variable ubuf  32 allot
variable up
: u. ( u -- )
  ubuf 32 + up !                 \ build digits from the end of the buffer
  begin
    dup 10 mod  [char] 0 +       ( u digit-char )
    up @ 1-  dup up !  c!         ( store digit, predecrement up )
    10 /
    dup 0=
  until
  drop
  up @   ubuf 32 + over -   type ;
